{"tool_name":"the-visualisation-tooklit-(vtk)","contents":["neubias"],"fetched_metadata":{"neubias":{"body":[{"format":"basic_html","processed":"<p>VTK is an open-source software system for image processing, 3D graphics, volume rendering and visualization. VTK includes many advanced algorithms (e.g., surface reconstruction, implicit modeling, decimation) and rendering techniques (e.g., hardware-accelerated volume rendering, LOD control).</p>\n\n<p>VTK is used by academicians for teaching and research; by government research institutions such as Los Alamos National Lab in the US or CINECA in Italy; and by many commercial firms who use VTK to build or extend products.</p>\n\n<p>The origin of VTK is with the textbook \"The Visualization Toolkit, an Object-Oriented Approach to 3D Graphics\" originally published by Prentice Hall and now published by Kitware, Inc. (Third Edition ISBN 1-930934-07-6). VTK has grown (since its initial release in 1994) to a world-wide user base in the commercial, academic, and research communities.</p>","summary":"","value":"<p dir=\"auto\">VTK is an open-source software system for image processing, 3D graphics, volume rendering and visualization. VTK includes many advanced algorithms (e.g., surface reconstruction, implicit modeling, decimation) and rendering techniques (e.g., hardware-accelerated volume rendering, LOD control).</p>\r\n\r\n<p dir=\"auto\">VTK is used by academicians for teaching and research; by government research institutions such as Los Alamos National Lab in the US or CINECA in Italy; and by many commercial firms who use VTK to build or extend products.</p>\r\n\r\n<p dir=\"auto\">The origin of VTK is with the textbook \"The Visualization Toolkit, an Object-Oriented Approach to 3D Graphics\" originally published by Prentice Hall and now published by Kitware, Inc. (Third Edition ISBN 1-930934-07-6). VTK has grown (since its initial release in 1994) to a world-wide user base in the commercial, academic, and research communities.</p>\r\n"}],"changed":[{"format":"Y-m-d\\TH:i:sP","value":"2024-10-25T13:17:50+00:00"}],"created":[{"format":"Y-m-d\\TH:i:sP","value":"2024-10-25T13:11:37+00:00"}],"default_langcode":[{"value":true}],"attribution_instructions":[],"comments_2023":[{"cid":0,"comment_count":0,"last_comment_name":null,"last_comment_timestamp":1729861897,"last_comment_uid":4013,"status":2}],"free_tagging":[],"give_feedback_on_this_soft":[{"cid":0,"comment_count":0,"last_comment_name":null,"last_comment_timestamp":1729861897,"last_comment_uid":4013,"status":2}],"has_author":[{"value":"Kitware"}],"has_biological_terms":[],"has_comparison":[],"has_documentation":[{"options":[],"title":"VTK Documentation","uri":"https://docs.vtk.org/en/latest/"}],"has_doi":[],"has_entry_curator":[{"target_id":4013,"target_type":"user","target_uuid":"197784c3-93e2-42ba-b80d-c0e15956e33d","url":"/user/4013"}],"has_function":[{"target_id":4118,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"http://edamontology.org/operation__R9Vc5BV3ZMcLHEL6WEceXbY","url":"/rigid-registration"},{"target_id":4046,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"http://edamontology.org/operation_Image_thresholding","url":"/image-thresholding"},{"target_id":4045,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0337","url":"/visualisation"},{"target_id":4041,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"http://edamontology.org/operation_Image_visualisation","url":"/image-visualisation"},{"target_id":4758,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"http://edamontology.org/operation____Slice_rendering","url":"/slice-rendering"},{"target_id":4759,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"http://edamontology.org/operation____Surface_rendering","url":"/surface-rendering"},{"target_id":4198,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"http://edamontology.org/operation_Volume_rendering","url":"/volume-rendering"},{"target_id":4172,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"http://edamontology.org/operation_